.TH SYS_FDT 2
.SH NAME
sys_fdt \- a copy of the firmware's device tree (SYS_FDT)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_fdt(void *buf, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_fdt
copies the device tree the firmware gave the kernel (a flattened device
tree blob) into
.IR buf ,
on machines that have one: arm64 (QEMU's
.B virt
machine, the Raspberry Pis). Drivers find their devices in it, with
lib/fdt.c: registers, interrupts, clocks.
.PP
If
.I size
is smaller than the tree, nothing is copied and the tree's size is
returned: call again with a buffer that big.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The tree's size in bytes, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOSYS
No device tree (x86-64).
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I buf
is not writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_acpi (2),
.BR map_phys (2),
.BR irq_bind (2)
